[{:adr/related ["90-docs/adr/2607171700-kotobase-storage-protocol-layers.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607172200-kotoba-resource-protocol-0.2-addendum.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607161817-kotoba-lang-dtn-tcp-transport-operational.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607162135-kotoba-lang-io-libp2p-real-tcp-transport.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607175100-smtp-kotobase-net.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607060100-kotoba-lang-reverse-domain-rename-batch2.edn"], :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "adr-2607172210-kotobase-protocol-layer-extension", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/last_verified "2026-07-17", :adr/body "# ADR-2607172210: kotobase protocol-layer extension — WebDAV / OCI+ORAS / IPFS Pinning API / Nostr / SFTP\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-17\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n**Scope**: `kotoba-lang` org — 4 new repos + 1 extension to `kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols`\n**Depends on**: ADR-2607172200 (KRP 0.2 addendum — the identity mappings this ADR's handlers implement)\n\n## Context\n\n`kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols` (ADR-2607171700, landed the same day as this ADR) covers s3 /\nipfs-gateway / atproto / git as pure `.cljc` handlers `(handle ctx req) → resp` over the injected\n`kotobase.store/IStore`. The owner asked to extend kotobase.net's storage-service surface with\nWebDAV, OCI Distribution, ORAS, an IPFS Pinning Service API, Nostr, and SFTP.\n\nThis was scoped interactively. Two research findings changed the layout from the owner's initial\nframing, and were confirmed with the owner before this ADR was written:\n\n1. **ORAS is not a second wire protocol.** OCI Distribution Spec v1.1's Referrers API is the ORAS\n   protocol — ORAS is a client convention over the same `/v2/...` endpoints. One repo covers both.\n2. **IPFS already has three homes** in this workspace (`kotobase-protocols/ipfs.cljc` gateway logic,\n   the general `kotoba-lang/ipfs` implementation lib, and the public product surface\n   `gftdcojp/net-kotobase-ipfs` which owns the `ipfs.kotobase.net` hostname per ADR-2607072000). A\n   Pinning Service API addition is pure incremental extension of the same block space\n   `kotobase-protocols/ipfs.cljc` already reads/writes — it stays there rather than becoming a 4th\n   home. This is the one place this ADR deviates from the owner's otherwise explicit,\n   twice-confirmed \"5 individual repos\" instruction, using exactly the shared-block-space /\n   no-new-surface reasoning ADR-2607171700 itself used to reject per-surface repos for the original\n   four.\n\nEverything else honors the owner's explicit choice: WebDAV, OCI+ORAS, Nostr, and SFTP each get their\nown individual `kotoba-lang` repo rather than a namespace inside `kotobase-protocols`, even though\nWebDAV and OCI are HTTP-shaped like the existing four surfaces and could technically share the\nrouter.\n\n**SFTP transport / deploy-target note (recorded here, not resolved here):** the owner's stated intent\nwas literal WASM-guest execution (`kotoba wasm` / `clojurewasm`) on murakumo cloud for the SFTP\nserver. Per CLAUDE.md's own runtime-priority documentation, neither `kotoba wasm` (closed\nkototama-ABI host-import table) nor `clojurewasm` (FFI is import-free-module-only per upstream's own\nroadmap) currently exposes a host capability for inbound TCP listen or crypto primitives. The most\nrecent, most directly comparable precedent in this codebase — `kotoba-lang/dtn` and\n`kotoba-lang/io-libp2p` gaining real TCP transports (ADR-2607161817, ADR-2607162135, the day before\nthis ADR) — used plain nbb `.cljs` + Node's `node:net`/`node:crypto` core modules as an OS process,\nnot WASM-guest execution, for exactly this reason. This ADR records Phase 1 following that precedent\n(nbb process, zero npm deps) with true WASM-guest hosting named as an explicit, separately-ADR'd\nPhase 2 blocked on new `kotoba-core-contracts` host capabilities — flagged here for the owner to\nrevisit if Phase-1 WASM-guest hosting is still wanted despite the missing capability.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Repo / namespace layout\n\n| Item | Where | Name | Spec body |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WebDAV | new repo | `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-webdav` | IETF RFC 4918 |\n| OCI Distribution (+ORAS) | new repo | `kotoba-lang/org-oci-distribution` | Open Container Initiative; ORAS satisfied by the same v1.1 Referrers API, no separate repo |\n| IPFS Pinning Service API | extend `kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols` | new fn(s) in `ipfs.cljc` / sibling `ipfs_pinning.cljc` | flagged deviation above |\n| Nostr (NIP-01 relay + NIP-96/Blossom blobs) | new repo | `kotoba-lang/nostr` | no formal body → bare name (same reasoning that excluded \"openapi\" from ADR-2607060100's rename batch); two namespaces: `nostr.relay` (WebSocket), `nostr.blossom` (HTTP) |\n| SFTP | new repo | `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-sftp` | IETF secsh WG draft — **never ratified to RFC**, noted explicitly to avoid overclaiming (same discipline as ADR-2607060100's `json`/`dag-cbor`/`openapi` exclusions) |\n\nNaming uses `org-<body>-<spec>` reverse-domain form for new repos with a clear owning body, matching\nthe pattern already used for freshly-created (not just renamed) repos like `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-smtp`\nand `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn`.\n\n### Per-protocol Phase-1 scope\n\nEvery new repo takes the same `io.github.kotoba-lang/kotobase` git-sha `deps.edn` pin\n`kotobase-protocols` uses, and vendors/reuses the same tiny primitives (`http.cljc`-shaped\nring request/response, `hash.cljc`-shaped fingerprinting) rather than reinventing `kotobase.store`.\n\n- **WebDAV**: `OPTIONS`/`GET`/`PUT`/`DELETE`/`HEAD`/`PROPFIND` (Depth 0/1, minimal\n  `<D:multistatus>`)/`MKCOL`. Out of scope v0.1: `LOCK`/`UNLOCK`, `COPY`/`MOVE`, `PROPPATCH` —\n  documented in the README the same way `s3.cljc` documents its SigV4/multipart/versioning carve-out.\n- **OCI Distribution**: `GET /v2/` ping, `GET/HEAD` manifests, `PUT` manifests, `GET/HEAD` blobs,\n  monolithic `POST` blob upload (chunked `PATCH` upload deferred), `GET` tags/list. Bearer-token\n  challenge shape emitted, verification deferred to the deploy shell (same split as s3.cljc/SigV4).\n  README documents the ORAS-is-the-same-API finding.\n- **IPFS Pinning API** (`kotobase-protocols` extension): `POST /pins`, `GET /pins/{requestid}`,\n  `GET /pins` (list), `DELETE /pins/{requestid}` per the ipfs.github.io pinning-services-api-spec.\n  New `:kotobase.protocols/pins` collection for request status, reuses existing\n  `blocks/put-block!`/`get-block`.\n- **Nostr relay** (`nostr.relay`): NIP-01 `EVENT`/`REQ`/`CLOSE`/`EOSE`/`OK`/`NOTICE` over a\n  hand-rolled RFC 6455 WebSocket handshake (`node:net`+`node:crypto`, zero npm deps — same discipline\n  as the DTN TCP transport precedent), schnorr signature verification.\n- **Nostr blobs** (`nostr.blossom`): NIP-98 HTTP Auth event verification, `PUT /upload`,\n  `GET /<sha256>`, `DELETE /<sha256>` (BUD-01/02).\n- **SFTP** (`org-ietf-sftp`): Phase 1 core = full `.cljc` filesystem-semantics\n  (readdir/stat/open-read/write/rename/remove mapped onto an IStore collection, parallel in shape to\n  `s3.cljc`'s object mapping) — ships regardless of the transport question. Phase 1 transport = nbb\n  `.cljs` hand-rolled SSH (RFC 4253) over `node:net`+`node:crypto`, explicitly labeled experimental /\n  pending security review in the README — this is the highest-risk item in this ADR; hand-rolled SSH\n  transport framing is security-sensitive even with library-provided crypto primitives. True\n  WASM-guest hosting is an explicit, separately-ADR'd Phase 2.\n\n## Rejected\n\n- **Folding WebDAV/OCI/Nostr/SFTP into `kotobase-protocols`** — considered (it's the technically\n  cleaner extension of ADR-2607171700's own precedent for the HTTP-shaped ones), but the owner\n  explicitly and twice confirmed per-protocol repos for these four; only the IPFS Pinning API\n  (already-shared block space, zero new surface, no owner override needed to justify it — the exact\n  rationale ADR-2607171700 used) stays in `kotobase-protocols`.\n- **A separate `oras` repo** — ORAS is a client convention over OCI Distribution v1.1, not a second\n  server; documented in `org-oci-distribution`'s README instead of duplicated.\n- **A 4th IPFS-adjacent repo for pinning** — three homes already exist (`kotobase-protocols/ipfs.cljc`,\n  `kotoba-lang/ipfs`, `gftdcojp/net-kotobase-ipfs`); a 4th for pure incremental extension has no\n  technical justification.\n- **Phase-1 WASM-guest SFTP hosting** — no host capability exists today (`kotoba-core-contracts` has\n  no TCP-listen/crypto host imports); attempting it now would mean starting with an unscoped,\n  security-sensitive host-capability project instead of shippable SFTP core logic. Phase 1 follows\n  the `kotoba-lang/dtn` nbb/`node:net` precedent instead; WASM-guest hosting is named as a separate\n  future Phase 2 ADR.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `nbb bin/run_tests.cljs` green in each new/extended repo before considering that repo's Phase 1 done\n  (matches `kotobase-protocols`'s own 24-test/87-assertion bar).\n- `nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs --check` clean after all 4 new manifest entries land.\n- Manual smoke per protocol against `kotobase.local/local-store` (same pattern as\n  `kotobase-protocols`'s README `router/handle` REPL example).\n\n## References\n\n- ADR-2607171700 (kotobase storage/protocol layers — the base pattern this extends)\n- ADR-2607172200 (KRP 0.2 addendum — identity mappings this ADR's handlers implement)\n- ADR-2607161817, ADR-2607162135 (`kotoba-lang/dtn`/`io-libp2p` real TCP transport — the SFTP Phase-1\n  transport precedent)\n- ADR-2607175100 (`smtp.kotobase.net` — the sibling precedent for phasing a wire protocol a\n  serverless deploy shell can't natively speak)\n- ADR-2607060100 (reverse-domain rename batch 2 — naming discipline this ADR follows for new repos)\n", :db/id -1, :adr/authoritative true, :adr/title "ADR-2607172210: kotobase protocol-layer extension -- WebDAV / OCI+ORAS / IPFS Pinning API / Nostr / SFTP", :adr/source-format "edn"}]